menu "Elixcon Light Hub"

    config ZBC_ZIGBEE_CHANNEL
        int "Zigbee channel used to form the network"
        range 11 26
        default 20
        help
            IEEE 802.15.4 channel the coordinator forms its network on. Keep it far
            away from the 2.4 GHz Wi-Fi channel your router uses, because the C6
            time-shares a single radio between Wi-Fi and Zigbee:
            Wi-Fi ch 1 -> Zigbee 25/26, Wi-Fi 6 -> Zigbee 15/26, Wi-Fi 11 -> Zigbee 15.
            Only the default for the first network formed. Settings -> Zigbee network
            moves a formed network to another channel without re-pairing, and a new
            network is formed on the channel it was last moved to.

    config ZBC_MAX_DEVICES
        int "Maximum number of paired devices"
        range 1 128
        default 32

    config ZBC_MAX_ENDPOINTS_PER_DEVICE
        int "Maximum controllable endpoints stored per device"
        range 1 16
        default 4

    config ZBC_MAX_GROUPS
        int "Maximum number of light groups"
        range 1 32
        default 8

    config ZBC_MAX_GROUP_MEMBERS
        int "Maximum number of lights in one group"
        range 1 64
        default 16

    config ZBC_MAX_CURVES
        int "Maximum number of lights with a stored response curve"
        range 1 64
        default 16
        help
            Only lights that have been given a curve take a slot; the rest pass
            their brightness through unchanged.

    config ZBC_MAX_BUTTONS
        int "Maximum number of buttons, counting every button of every remote"
        range 1 16
        default 16

    config ZBC_MAX_LIGHTS
        int "Maximum number of lights wired to a pin"
        range 1 16
        default 8
        help
            Changing it changes nothing stored: the table is kept by count. Each
            wired light can be a group member like any Zigbee lamp.

    config ZBC_MAX_MATTER_LIGHTS
        int "Maximum number of Matter lamps"
        range 1 32
        default 16
        help
            Each light endpoint of a paired Matter device is one - a hub such as a
            Hue Bridge brings one per lamp behind it. Changing it changes nothing
            stored: the table is kept by count.

    config ZBC_MAX_MATTER_NODES
        int "Maximum number of paired Matter devices"
        range 1 16
        default 8
        help
            A Matter device - a lamp, or a hub with lamps behind it - holds one
            secure session and one subscription for as long as it is paired, which
            costs heap. A hub counts once, however many lamps it has.

    config ZBC_BUTTON_RULES
        int "Rules on one button, all its gestures together"
        range 12 32
        default 12
        help
            Each gesture is a short list of "when this, do that" rules, tried in
            order until one matches, and a rule can bring others along with "and".
            The gestures of one button share this many, so a wall switch with one
            gesture can use them all. Not below 12: that is what a button stored
            before the pool held (four gestures of three), and converting one must
            not lose a rule.
            Changing it changes the size of a stored button and discards the
            stored buttons unless BUTTON_BLOB_VERSION in button_db.c is bumped
            with it.

    config ZBC_MAX_AUTH_KEYS
        int "Maximum number of access keys"
        range 1 32
        default 8
        help
            Browser cookies and API keys share this table. It is stored by count,
            so changing the limit discards nothing.

    config ZBC_UNLOCK_GPIO
        int "Pin always watched for the failsafe unlock (-1 = none)"
        range -1 30
        default 9
        help
            Pressing a button 5 times, each press 0.5-2.5 s after the last, opens
            the web UI to everyone for ZBC_UNLOCK_WINDOW_S. Every configured button
            counts, GPIO and Zigbee alike; this pin counts even when nobody made a
            button of it, so a bare board can always be let back into. GPIO 9 is
            the BOOT button on the ESP32-C6 dev boards. Expected wired to GND; the
            internal pull-up is enabled.

    config ZBC_UNLOCK_WINDOW_S
        int "How long the failsafe keeps the web UI open, in seconds"
        range 30 900
        default 120
        help
            The window also closes as soon as a browser is issued a key, so it is
            open for as short a time as the person standing at the board needs.

    config ZBC_SETUP_HOTSPOT_S
        int "How long a button-started setup hotspot waits before going back, in seconds"
        range 120 3600
        default 600
        help
            Pressing a button 10 times in the failsafe rhythm restarts the
            coordinator into its setup hotspot while keeping the stored Wi-Fi.
            If no new network is saved in that time it restarts back onto the
            stored one. While a device is still connected to the hotspot it
            waits a minute at a time, up to three times this long in total.

    config ZBC_AP_PASSWORD
        string "SoftAP password used during provisioning (empty = open network)"
        default ""
        help
            Leave empty for an open provisioning AP. If set, must be at least 8
            characters.

    config ZBC_CONFIG_BACKUP
        bool "Configuration backup and restore"
        default y
        help
            GET /api/backup downloads the settings and tables as a file - device
            names, rooms, curves, buttons, wired lights, Matter lamps, Adaptive
            Lighting, and on request the Wi-Fi password and the access keys'
            hashes - and POST /api/restore gives one back, applied at the next
            boot. Settings shows it as the Backup card. Never the Zigbee network
            or the Matter fabrics. Off, neither route exists, the page has no
            Backup card, and the boot does not look for a staged restore. Costs
            about 10 KB of flash.

    config ZBC_HEAP_WATCH
        bool "Record how far each web request takes the heap down"
        default n
        help
            Brackets every HTTP request with the heap's local-minimum monitor and
            adds `routes`, `lows_between_requests` and `marks` to `heap` in
            /api/status: each route's shallowest, mean and deepest drop below the
            free heap at its start, new boot-wide lows (between requests looked
            for once a second, inside one named with its route; each kept with
            the events just before it),
            and timed Zigbee, Matter, Adaptive Lighting and Wi-Fi events to match
            those against. A diagnostic: it costs about 2.5 KB of RAM, a small
            allocation per request and about 3 KB of status JSON. Off, `heap`
            carries only free, min_free and largest.

    config ZBC_HEAP_WATCH_TASKS
        bool "Also count the heap each task holds"
        depends on ZBC_HEAP_WATCH
        select HEAP_TASK_TRACKING
        default n
        help
            Adds `tasks` to `heap` in /api/status - per task, the bytes in blocks
            it allocated that are not freed yet, its peak, and what it held ten
            minutes after boot - to each low the three tasks whose holding rose
            most in the second before it (in the request, for a low inside one),
            and to each mark
            what the task leaving it held. A task that keeps growing is a leak;
            the one that rose is what took the heap down.

            It turns on HEAP_TASK_TRACKING for the 4-byte owner that option puts
            in every block, and replaces that option's own bookkeeping, which
            keeps a list entry per allocation (about 24 bytes each) and walks it
            on every free. What is left costs 4 bytes per block and about 2 KB.

    config ZBC_CRASH_TEST
        bool "Crash on request, to test the crash record"
        default n
        help
            Lets POST /api/restart take {"crash": "abort"}, {"crash": "fault"} or
            {"crash": "stack"}: half a second later a task of its own calls
            abort(), reads an unmapped address or overruns its stack (the
            hardware stack guard's fault), and the next boot shows what
            crash_log.c recorded as `last_crash` in /api/status. Refused while
            the image is on trial, which a crash would roll back. For a test
            build only.

    config ZBC_WLED
        bool "WLED interface: stream to Hue colour lamps"
        default y
        help
            Lets Hyperion.ng, HyperHDR, LedFx and other WLED clients drive one
            room's Hue colour lamps as a WLED strip, through Philips'
            entertainment frames (cluster 0xFC01): DDP on UDP 4048, WLED's own
            UDP protocols on 21324, WLED's /json API and the _wled._tcp mDNS
            service. Settings shows it as the WLED interface card, off until
            somebody switches it on there. Off, none of it is built: no ports,
            no /json routes, no card, no `wled` in /api/settings and /api/status
            and no `ent` in `zigbee`. The interface's settings stay in NVS and in
            a backup, so a build with it finds them again. Costs about 16 KB of
            flash and 1 KB of RAM.

    config ZBC_WLED_WS
        bool "WLED apps: WLED's WebSocket /ws"
        depends on ZBC_WLED
        default n
        select HTTPD_WS_SUPPORT
        select HTTPD_WS_POST_HANDSHAKE_CB_SUPPORT
        help
            The WLED apps (iOS, Android) show a device online only while its
            WebSocket /ws is open. With this, the WLED interface serves one: the
            state as it opens, and after each command the app sends there. The
            apps can then switch the WLED interface's room. Hyperion, HyperHDR
            and LedFx do not need it. Costs about 7.4 KB of flash, the HTTP
            server's WebSocket support included.

            Switching it off again in an existing sdkconfig leaves
            HTTPD_WS_SUPPORT and HTTPD_WS_POST_HANDSHAKE_CB_SUPPORT on: an
            sdkconfig keeps what a select once set. Turn those two off by hand,
            or the image keeps 7 KB of WebSocket code with no route using it.

endmenu
